{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-discipline", "file": "references/full-line-stripe-analysis.md", "content": "# Full-line stripe detection and output validation

When demos of mixed geometry may have a shared rule, inventory full-width uniform rows and full-height uniform columns independently before projecting anything. A task may color complete structural lines while preserving every non-line cell, including noise or markers near the line. Distinguish genuinely uniform lines from near-uniform rows/columns with scattered markers; a near-stripe can be negative evidence against simple 'mostly uniform' selection.

Record deltas for every cell on candidate lines: demos can indicate either recoloring only the original stripe color or overwriting the entire line, so don't assume one. Compare horizontal-only, vertical-only, and both-orientation rules against every demo and preserved off-line markers. Apply only the rule that explains all examples, then mechanically validate output dimensions and row widths. This is a discriminating checklist, not a solved transformation for any specific task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-discipline/references/full-line-stripe-analysis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-lessons", "file": "references/noisy-motif-projection.md", "content": "# Sparse motif projection: review lesson

A demo had a diagonal multi-color marker pattern, while the test contained a compact related motif. The response guessed a single local color adjustment without establishing the transformation. Generalizable lesson: derive the full rule from all changed and unchanged demo coordinates, then apply that operation to the entire test motif; do not preserve most input cells merely because they appear unrelated. Where the demo adds diagonal runs, enumerate the rays and their bounds explicitly and distinguish them from row/column continuation or marker replacement. This is a caution about the analysis method, not a confirmed rule for the particular puzzle.
